# Base configuration defaults, applied to every target.
#
# ESP-IDF picks this file up automatically, then layers
# sdkconfig.defaults.<IDF_TARGET> on top of it (see
# tools/cmake/kconfig.cmake). So the whole build is just:
#
#     idf.py set-target esp32      # or esp32s2 / esp32s3 / esp32c3
#     idf.py build
#
# The per-target files hold the real settings. This file has to exist even
# though it is nearly empty: IDF only looks for the .<IDF_TARGET> variants of
# entries that are already in SDKCONFIG_DEFAULTS, and that list starts out
# containing this file and nothing else. Delete it and every per-target file is
# silently ignored, which looks exactly like a build that worked.
#
# This replaces the old instruction to copy sdkconfig.<chip>.4mb over
# `sdkconfig` by hand. That was easy to get wrong -- `sdkconfig` is generated
# and git-ignored, so a build would quietly rewrite your copy, and switching
# chips without remembering to re-copy produced a binary for the wrong target.
#
# Nothing is set here on purpose. Every option the project actually needs is
# currently identical across all four targets but is already written out in
# each per-target file, so hoisting any of it here would only create a second
# place to look. Add a setting here when it genuinely applies to every target
# and is not already stated per-target.
